# Dependencies
node_modules/
target/

# pnpm-lock.yaml is deliberately tracked: `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`
# needs it, and a harness whose whole claim is reproducibility cannot ignore
# the file that pins its dependencies.

# Build artifacts
*.node
# Anchored deliberately. Unanchored, `dist/` and `coverage/` and `sessions/`
# match at ANY depth -- which is how benchmark evidence came to be excluded by
# a rule meant for build output, leaving published results citing traces a
# clone would never have.
/dist/

# pnpm store
.pnpm-store/

# Runtime
/sessions/
*.log
*.jsonl

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Session snapshots and audit trails are machine-local and hold
# conversation text. They must never enter the repository.
.gnomon-sessions/
.gnomon-audit/
# Same reasoning: .gnomon-out/ holds tool output too large for the context
# window, and .gnomon-jobs/ the logs of backgrounded commands. Both are
# whatever the repository happened to print, kept so the model can read it
# back instead of re-running the command.
.gnomon-out/
.gnomon-jobs/

# Local API key drop — never commit. Keys belong in the machine-local
# credential store (`gnomon key set`), never in the repo.
api.txt
*.key
.env
.gnomon-loops/

# Transient scratch the write-path unit tests drop at the config root.
# They are test output, never source; the tests assert on the write outcome,
# not the file's persistence.
/x.txt
/y.txt

# Python bytecode (benchmarks/ is Python).
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/

# Root-level research scratch (working notes, not repo content — move to docs/ to keep).
/Common Denominators of Top-Performing AI Agent Harnesses.md
/Gnomon Harness Benchmark Post-Mortem Review and Path to SOTA.md

# Benchmark evidence is the exception to *.log / *.jsonl above. A published
# result that cites a trace a clone does not have is not evidence, it is an
# assertion -- and four results/ READMEs cited files excluded by those two
# lines. The traces are small and they are the whole point of keeping them.
!benchmarks/results/**/*.jsonl
!benchmarks/results/**/*.log

# `pnpm run coverage` is a documented contributor step, and it rewrites
# these on every run. Sixteen of them were tracked, so following the
# documented workflow left every contributor with a dirty tree.
/coverage/

# Nothing inside a .gnomon/ surface is ever ignored.
#
# The surface is content-hashed, and `gnomon surface hash` walks every file
# under .gnomon/ -- so a stray file there changes the identity every session
# record and audit record is traced by. The rules above hid ten kinds of stray
# file from `git status` while the hash moved underneath them. Measured
# 2026-09-02 with `git check-ignore -v`, all ten IGNORED before this stanza:
#
#   .gnomon/scratch.log            <- *.log
#   .gnomon/run.jsonl              <- *.jsonl
#   .gnomon/sessions/x.toml        <- sessions/
#   .gnomon/dist/a.txt             <- dist/
#   .gnomon/coverage/x             <- coverage/
#   .gnomon/.env                   <- .env
#   .gnomon/a.key                  <- *.key
#   .gnomon/__pycache__/x.pyc      <- __pycache__/
#   .gnomon/target/x               <- target/
#   packages/gnomon-cli/.gnomon/x.log  <- *.log
#
# A clean `git status` over a moved hash is the worst available combination:
# the operator's one signal that the surface changed says nothing.
#
# The directory form is needed as well as the file form. Git will not descend
# into an excluded directory, so re-including `.gnomon/sessions/x.toml` alone
# cannot work -- `.gnomon/sessions` itself has to be un-excluded first, and
# `**/.gnomon/**` matches the directory too. The leading `**/` covers nested
# surfaces (packages/gnomon-cli/.gnomon/), not only the one at the root.
#
# This deliberately un-ignores .gnomon/.env and .gnomon/*.key. That is the
# intended direction: a key inside a hashed, committable directory is a finding
# the auditor already calls fatal, and it must be VISIBLE so someone deletes and
# rotates it -- not quietly hidden from `git status` while it sits in the tree.
#
# .gnomon-sessions/, .gnomon-audit/ and .gnomon-loops/ are unaffected: they are
# different path components, not children of .gnomon/, and they stay ignored.
!**/.gnomon/**
